.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-3.0-or-later
.Dd October 28, 2026
.Dt NQCTEQ 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nqcteq
.Nd compare two buffers in constant time
.Sh LIBRARY
libnoqtis, \-lnoqtis
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In noqtis.h
.Ft int
.Fn nqcteq "const void *a" "const void *b" "size_t len"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn nqcteq
compares the
.Fa len
bytes at
.Fa a
and
.Fa b
in time that depends only on
.Fa len ,
not on their contents or on where they first differ.
Use it for MAC tags, shared secrets and anything else an attacker must not
learn byte by byte; use
.Xr memcmp 3
only for public data.
.Pp
It does not order the buffers: it tells only whether they are equal.
Neither pointer is checked:
.Fa a
and
.Fa b
must point to
.Fa len
readable bytes, and may be
.Dv NULL
only when
.Fa len
is 0.
The bytes are ORed together as a ^ b and the result turned into 0 or 1
without a branch on it.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
1 when the buffers are equal, 0 otherwise.
A
.Fa len
of 0 gives 1.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
uint8_t tag[32];
if (nqhmac(NQS256, key, keylen, msg, msglen, tag, sizeof tag) == NQOK &&
    nqcteq(tag, received, sizeof tag))
  accept();
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr noqtis 3 ,
.Xr nqhash 3 ,
.Xr nqwipe 3
.Pp
.Pa proofs/lean/Ct.lean
of the source tree proves the word operations of
.Fn nqcteq ,
.Fn nqctmask ,
.Fn nqctsel
and
.Fn nqcmov
correct for every 32-bit word, as Lean models of the C expressions.
